1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:01,960
Shipyards.

2
00:00:01,961 --> 00:00:08,680
They're giant waterside construction sites, where the extraordinary take shape.

3
00:00:08,681 --> 00:00:18,400
Places where the largest of machines are constructed, retrofitted, and repaired.

4
00:00:18,401 --> 00:00:22,960
In times of peace, they create the vessels that carry commerce and international trade.

5
00:00:22,961 --> 00:00:33,700
And during war, they build the mightiest weapons the world has ever known.

6
00:00:33,701 --> 00:00:40,160
On the shores of the Chesapeake Bay sits one of the United States' largest and oldest shipyards.

7
00:00:40,161 --> 00:00:46,320
The sprawling 500-acre Northrop Grumman Newport News facility is situated on nearly two miles

8
00:00:46,321 --> 00:00:51,320
of waterfront.

9
00:00:51,321 --> 00:00:56,294
Workers at the facility are responsible for building and maintaining U.S.

10
00:00:56,295 --> 00:00:57,520
aircraft carriers,

11
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:01,260
the most complicated warships to ever rule the waves.

12
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:06,220
When we bring people into the shipyard, invariably that's the first thing.

13
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:08,280
When you look at an aircraft carrier, they walk up to it,

14
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:11,400
and it's hard to conceptualize that what they're looking at is a ship

15
00:01:11,401 --> 00:01:14,660
because it takes up the entire horizon.

16
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:20,120
And when you think about putting that ship together

17
00:01:20,121 --> 00:01:22,880
starting with a plate of steel or that piece of pipe,

18
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:25,520
it is an awesome challenge,

19
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:28,880
and it is a deep sense of pride when you get to the finish line.

20
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:32,160
Carrier construction can take nearly eight years

21
00:01:32,161 --> 00:01:36,140
and relies on nearly every muscle that the shipyard has to offer.

22
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:47,640
Because multi-ton ship parts can't be picked up

23
00:01:47,641 --> 00:01:49,060
at the local home improvement center,

24
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:52,080
Shipyards have to build everything they can't buy.

25
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:56,300
Shipyard is a huge place.

26
00:01:56,760 --> 00:02:00,800
At one point in time, we had the capacity to do everything that was in the ship.

27
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:04,020
Over the years, we've become more specialized.

28
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:08,000
We've retained certain core capabilities in the shipyard.

29
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:10,060
One example is our foundry.

30
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:32,120
Workers within the over 250,000 square foot foundry pour up to 2,900 degree Fahrenheit

31
00:02:32,121 --> 00:02:39,280
molten metal into castings to form many of the ship's unique pieces.

32
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:44,160
We also have other shops that make up the individual components.

33
00:02:44,161 --> 00:02:46,560
We have electrical component shop.

34
00:02:46,561 --> 00:02:48,060
We have pipe shop.

35
00:02:48,061 --> 00:02:52,560
We have a machine shop capable of machining anything you would ever want to machine.

36
00:02:52,561 --> 00:02:57,960
All these shops are equipped with the state of the art equipment.

37
00:02:57,961 --> 00:03:04,460
But all these shops could be part of any huge industrial work site.

38
00:03:04,461 --> 00:03:08,960
What sets shipyards apart is the size of the material being created.

39
00:03:08,961 --> 00:03:14,660
and the fact that the final product must eventually find its way to water.

40
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:21,780
Enormous dry docks are how many shipyards deal with both problems.

41
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:25,980
I'm standing on top of the intermediate gate

42
00:03:25,981 --> 00:03:29,060
in the largest dry dock in the Western Hemisphere.

43
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:31,640
Here at Northrop Grumman, Newport News,

44
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:35,320
the purpose of this intermediate gate gives us the option

45
00:03:35,321 --> 00:03:38,020
to divide this dry dock up into two sections.

46
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:45,720
It's 2,173 feet long and 250 foot wide with a depth of about 40 feet.

47
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:49,860
What you see here is just half of this massive dry dock.

48
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,720
The other half is behind me.

49
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:56,620
A dry dock is a concrete berth with a watertight gate at one end.

50
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:03,000
Inside these often massive enclosures, shipyard workers both repair and build vessels.

51
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:08,800
During ship construction, the dry dock is the focal point of the yard.

52
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:12,040
where towering preassembled modules are joined together.

53
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:18,720
But as with all modern shipyards, the building process starts small.

54
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:24,380
The construction of the components for the carrier start in the steel fab facility

55
00:04:24,381 --> 00:04:30,700
where we built small pieces, bulkheads, girders, foundations,

56
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:34,340
that come together to make up preassemblies.

57
00:04:34,341 --> 00:04:43,060
Those bigger pre-assemblies are then brought together in another shop to make sub-assemblies,

58
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:44,060
which are a little bigger.

59
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:51,980
These can get up to 50 to 150 tons.

60
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:03,440
Over the course of months, workers weld, fit, and connect multiple sub-assemblies into even

61
00:05:03,441 --> 00:05:12,120
bigger modules, the largest of which are called superlifts and can weigh in at a staggering

62
00:05:12,121 --> 00:05:12,944
900 tons.

63
00:05:12,945 --> 00:05:20,180
To move these enormous ship elements, workers make use of one of the shipyard's

64
00:05:20,181 --> 00:05:23,839
most conspicuous pieces of equipment, a crane.

65
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:27,100
But like everything else in the shipyard,

66
00:05:27,101 --> 00:05:30,880
these construction tools are on a supermassive scale.

67
00:05:30,881 --> 00:05:34,160
At the far end of the shipyard, there's a crane.

68
00:05:34,161 --> 00:05:37,120
We call it the 900-ton crane, and we call it the 900-ton crane

69
00:05:37,121 --> 00:05:40,120
because that's the capacity for a lift.

70
00:05:40,121 --> 00:05:42,220
And you say, well, what other business

71
00:05:42,221 --> 00:05:43,960
would require such a crane?

72
00:05:43,961 --> 00:05:45,380
And there probably aren't any.

73
00:05:45,381 --> 00:05:47,500
Those kinds of things are unique to shipyards.

74
00:05:50,940 --> 00:05:53,260
The crane's 540 feet wide.

75
00:05:53,261 --> 00:05:55,840
It's 290 feet tall.

76
00:05:55,841 --> 00:05:59,760
It weighs approximately a little over 10 million pounds.

77
00:05:59,761 --> 00:06:03,080
has 37 miles of electrical cable.

78
00:06:03,081 --> 00:06:05,140
At full power, it uses enough power

79
00:06:05,141 --> 00:06:08,580
to supply 114 houses.

80
00:06:08,581 --> 00:06:11,080
People don't really understand how big the crane is.

81
00:06:11,081 --> 00:06:13,700
The letters on the crane are three stories tall,

82
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:17,100
and it takes 45 gallons of paint to paint them.

83
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:25,460
The crane operator sits over 200 feet above the ground,

84
00:06:25,461 --> 00:06:28,420
while crews below attach the massive cables

85
00:06:28,421 --> 00:06:29,420
to the superlifts.

86
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:37,240
Gantry cranes like this one are ideal for shipyard construction

87
00:06:37,241 --> 00:06:39,740
because they carry heavier loads which hang directly

88
00:06:39,741 --> 00:06:43,880
below the structure, as opposed to boom cranes, which

89
00:06:43,881 --> 00:06:45,400
hang the loads from the end of an arm.

90
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:51,500
Gantry cranes also allow the loads to be moved great distances

91
00:06:51,501 --> 00:06:56,420
due to their rolling base, a feature that allows the superlift

92
00:06:56,421 --> 00:06:58,760
to be placed anywhere inside the massive dry dock.

93
00:07:01,060 --> 00:07:03,060
But in spite of the gigantic tools,

94
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:06,520
the shipyard's greatest attribute remains the craftspeople

95
00:07:06,521 --> 00:07:09,980
that have learned the art and skill of making ships.

96
00:07:10,740 --> 00:07:12,320
It's a large number of folks.

97
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:15,300
Right now at Northrop Grumman Newport News,

98
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:16,940
we've got about 18,000 employees.

99
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:20,580
It takes a lot of time and a lot of talent to bring all this together.

100
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:24,020
And this is something that's unique to our industry,

101
00:07:24,021 --> 00:07:28,020
especially when you consider the size of the product that we're producing.

102
00:07:28,021 --> 00:07:34,020
The vessels the craftspeople create are the end product of a long evolutionary process

103
00:07:34,021 --> 00:07:37,020
spurred through the ages by revolutions in the shipyard.

104
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:44,020
For ancient civilizations, shipyards like these didn't exist.

105
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:52,020
And scientists who look for these early construction sites engage in a frustrating task.

106
00:07:52,021 --> 00:07:56,500
task we know they're supposed to be there we haven't found one yet or at least we don't think

107
00:07:56,501 --> 00:08:02,660
we found one so we're waiting for the next great discovery the breakthrough archaeologists are

108
00:08:02,661 --> 00:08:06,740
incredible optimists and every time i go on the field i hope this is the time i'd like to find one

109
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:11,860
a good number of the ancient sites that we're interested in where we know there were shipyards

110
00:08:11,861 --> 00:08:17,860
are now still functioning as harbors so you've got the modern overlaying the ancient and the second

111
00:08:17,861 --> 00:08:21,260
The second thing is they were never very major installations.

112
00:08:21,261 --> 00:08:24,980
Ships in antiquity were built on the beach, simply propping up a hull that was going to

113
00:08:24,981 --> 00:08:25,980
be worked on.

114
00:08:25,981 --> 00:08:29,760
That's still being done in places like Turkey and Greece today.

115
00:08:29,761 --> 00:08:32,100
And that's what would have been done in antiquity.

116
00:08:32,101 --> 00:08:34,860
But some evidence of early shipyards does exist.

117
00:08:34,861 --> 00:08:39,540
For the Egyptians, clues to theirs are appropriately buried with their dead.

118
00:08:39,541 --> 00:08:44,900
We're very fortunate to have a number of depictions of boats and ships being built,

119
00:08:44,901 --> 00:08:47,820
presumably along the Nile.

120
00:08:47,821 --> 00:08:49,680
in shipyards by definition.

121
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:52,520
There's one painted relief in a tomb

122
00:08:52,521 --> 00:08:54,260
belonging to a guy by the name of T

123
00:08:54,261 --> 00:08:56,600
that dates to the latter part

124
00:08:56,601 --> 00:08:57,820
of the third millennium B.C.

125
00:08:58,180 --> 00:09:00,980
You have people lobbing the branches off logs.

126
00:09:01,260 --> 00:09:03,960
Then you have planks with people sitting on top of them

127
00:09:03,961 --> 00:09:06,360
cutting mortise and tenon joints into the planks.

128
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:09,480
And they would do this with a chisel and a mallet.

129
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:12,000
Throughout the Greek and Roman empires,

130
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:13,800
the Middle Ages,

131
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:16,300
and into 15th century China

132
00:09:16,301 --> 00:09:19,680
where massive 400-foot-long wooden ships were constructed.

133
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:22,320
Design and vessel size were altered greatly,

134
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:28,080
but the yards, techniques, and tools used to build them remained little changed.

135
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:31,860
For Italian shipwrights and European master carpenters of the Renaissance,

136
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:34,800
the two most important attributes of a shipyard

137
00:09:34,801 --> 00:09:38,640
were a gently sloped piece of land and a deepwater channel.

138
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:43,100
In the yard, workers used a chopping tool known as an adze

139
00:09:43,101 --> 00:09:45,520
to hue and shape the internal timber frames.

140
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:49,440
while the mechanical advantage provided by block and tackle,

141
00:09:49,441 --> 00:09:51,100
coupled with animal power,

142
00:09:51,101 --> 00:09:55,340
helped shipbuilders to lift heavy masts and yard arms into place.

143
00:09:55,341 --> 00:09:58,440
The hammers were there, the chisels were there, the azzes were there,

144
00:09:58,441 --> 00:09:59,940
but nothing very complicated.

145
00:09:59,941 --> 00:10:03,660
You have to realize that this is a labor force that very often had very little training.

146
00:10:03,661 --> 00:10:08,460
Shipwrights themselves, there were some people who built ships that were in fact extremely well trained,

147
00:10:08,461 --> 00:10:10,700
but they often worked with a lot of people who weren't.

148
00:10:10,701 --> 00:10:12,460
You had to keep things very simple.

149
00:10:12,461 --> 00:10:15,800
And I think sometimes we have a tendency, because of the world we live in,

150
00:10:15,801 --> 00:10:18,800
and to think that only complexity will get results.

151
00:10:18,801 --> 00:10:20,600
Well, that's not the case.

152
00:10:20,601 --> 00:10:22,760
By the end of the 18th century,

153
00:10:22,761 --> 00:10:25,800
trade began to fuel industrialization,

154
00:10:25,801 --> 00:10:27,120
and the goods being carried

155
00:10:27,121 --> 00:10:29,280
and the waterways on which they traveled

156
00:10:29,281 --> 00:10:31,760
began to change ship design.

157
00:10:31,761 --> 00:10:33,980
Growing nations realized their prosperity

158
00:10:33,981 --> 00:10:36,320
was tied to the ships they sailed,

159
00:10:36,321 --> 00:10:38,720
and it fell to the shipyards and craftspeople

160
00:10:38,721 --> 00:10:40,320
to help build that future.

161
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:44,740
During the 17th century,

162
00:10:44,741 --> 00:10:47,100
the British were one of the world's leading shipbuilders.

163
00:10:48,100 --> 00:10:50,160
Both an island nation and world power,

164
00:10:50,980 --> 00:10:53,520
Britain depended on its navy for international trade

165
00:10:53,521 --> 00:10:55,760
and to defend its vast holdings.

166
00:10:57,020 --> 00:10:59,440
Ships built in English port towns such as Portsmouth

167
00:10:59,441 --> 00:11:02,780
were constructed and repaired in some of the best shipyards of the day.

168
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:06,920
Soon after the creation of the Royal Navy in 1670,

169
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:10,640
the British government built the world's first stone dry dock.

170
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:12,720
Also known as a graving dock,

171
00:11:12,721 --> 00:11:17,800
It allowed the British to repair their large ships of the line in a permanent and dry facility.

172
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:22,240
Those docks are still in existence today and are still being used.

173
00:11:22,780 --> 00:11:26,140
One actually is the permanent home of the HMS Victory.

174
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:33,140
Roughly 50 years after its founding, the burgeoning United States commissioned the first American graving docks.

175
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:41,440
In 1827, engineers began building two, one in Boston, the second on the Elizabeth River in Portsmouth, Virginia.

176
00:11:42,180 --> 00:11:46,820
The civil engineering feat was one of the greatest the fledgling nation had ever undertaken.

177
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:54,580
To dig this hole in the ground in 1827 was an extraordinary event.

178
00:11:55,220 --> 00:12:02,440
They had to excavate this hole that was 400 feet long, approximately 20 feet deep at least.

179
00:12:02,441 --> 00:12:05,300
Then below that, they had to drive the piles down.

180
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:07,440
Piles were like telephone poles.

181
00:12:07,441 --> 00:12:11,520
On top of these piles, then, they laid cut granite stone.

182
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:15,200
For early shipyards that specialized in repair,

183
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:18,380
the dry dock was their single greatest physical asset.

184
00:12:19,440 --> 00:12:22,280
But other American yards in the business of building new ships

185
00:12:22,281 --> 00:12:26,500
relied more on a form of shipyard natural selection for success.

186
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:31,840
The history of shipbuilding in the United States goes back to the colonial time

187
00:12:31,841 --> 00:12:38,120
because the East Coast was fortunate in having many small protected harbors,

188
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:40,340
tidal areas where wooden ships could be built,

189
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:46,740
and a great store of timber standing right nearby that could be used to build these ships.

190
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:49,844
In the U.S.

191
00:12:49,845 --> 00:12:55,440
from 1820 to 1890, more ships were built in Maine than anywhere in the United States.

192
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:58,740
And due to unique geographic conditions,

193
00:12:58,741 --> 00:13:02,300
A majority of that building was done near the small town of Bath.

194
00:13:02,301 --> 00:13:06,240
The reasons for it included the availability of natural resources,

195
00:13:06,241 --> 00:13:09,840
a skilled labor pool, and as importantly,

196
00:13:09,841 --> 00:13:14,540
a long stretch of the river from Hospital Point to Day's Ferry,

197
00:13:14,541 --> 00:13:18,340
three miles where the western shore, the Bath side of the river,

198
00:13:18,341 --> 00:13:23,340
was far enough away from the river to accommodate the length of the vessels being built.

199
00:13:23,341 --> 00:13:27,040
Vessels couldn't be built on the opposite shore, the Arousic shore,

200
00:13:27,041 --> 00:13:30,260
because of how steep the shore rises up out of the river.

201
00:13:31,440 --> 00:13:35,740
The coastline around Bath allowed building sites such as the Persean Smalls shipyard

202
00:13:35,741 --> 00:13:40,660
to create mammoth wooden vessels, some of the biggest to ever take to the waves.

203
00:13:41,300 --> 00:13:47,000
One of the most important ingredients of a shipbuilding site, like this here at Persean Smalls Southways,

204
00:13:47,240 --> 00:13:50,920
was finding an area, first of all, big enough to build the vessel one wanted to build,

205
00:13:51,220 --> 00:13:54,000
and secondly, one that had just the right slope to the land.

206
00:13:54,001 --> 00:13:59,280
It's hard to see, but this land here slopes about three-quarters of an inch to a foot down to the river,

207
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:02,160
just right to launch the vessels that were built here.

208
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:06,440
The launching of a ship had changed little since antiquity.

209
00:14:07,620 --> 00:14:10,800
Workers built the vessels on top of wooden rails, known as ways.

210
00:14:11,720 --> 00:14:15,860
This is a model we use to demonstrate launching the completed wooden vessel.

211
00:14:16,660 --> 00:14:21,520
It would have been built right here in its position on top of the tracks or launching ways,

212
00:14:21,521 --> 00:14:28,480
but shortly before launching, a cradle would have been constructed on top of the ways between it and the bottom of the vessel.

213
00:14:29,100 --> 00:14:35,400
Just before launching, the two halves of the cradle would be snugged up against the base of the hull

214
00:14:35,401 --> 00:14:43,080
by wedges driven in between the two halves of the cradle.

215
00:14:43,680 --> 00:14:49,640
And then at just the right moment, at precisely slack high water, when there's no movement in the river at all,

216
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:54,080
the keel blocks would start being knocked out from underneath the keel,

217
00:14:54,420 --> 00:14:56,940
and finally, at just the right moment,

218
00:14:57,480 --> 00:15:03,220
the vessel would slide, hopefully, into the river.

219
00:15:06,780 --> 00:15:10,400
The men who split out the blocks from under the tons of teetering ship

220
00:15:10,401 --> 00:15:12,900
had one of the most dangerous jobs in the yard.

221
00:15:13,740 --> 00:15:17,540
But they also had an up-front seat for the shipyard's most awesome spectacle.

222
00:15:17,541 --> 00:15:23,820
If one could have been standing here then and looking behind me and watching one of these big vessels take shape,

223
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:32,380
ultimately at the right moment one would have watched it smoke past us with great thunder and applause and smoking timbers as it took to the Kennebec River.

224
00:15:34,540 --> 00:15:40,280
Besides a near-perfect natural environment, Maine had another shipbuilding asset, its people.

225
00:15:41,340 --> 00:15:45,740
19th century shipyards were less of a physical establishment than a local gathering place.

226
00:15:45,741 --> 00:15:50,740
As had always been the case, whole communities were part of the shipbuilding process.

227
00:15:50,741 --> 00:15:57,740
In communities where there were many shipbuilding facilities, there were often tradesmen who

228
00:15:57,741 --> 00:16:01,740
set up shops that supplied services to many different shipyards.

229
00:16:01,741 --> 00:16:06,740
For example, a painter might go to paint vessels at various shipyards.

230
00:16:06,741 --> 00:16:13,740
A shipcarver might produce lots of ship carvings, figureheads, trail boards, other carvings,

231
00:16:13,741 --> 00:16:16,140
to decorate vessels built in many different shipyards.

232
00:16:16,780 --> 00:16:25,260
This is basically from the beginning of time to the end of wooden shipbuilding.

233
00:16:28,060 --> 00:16:33,900
But one change during the 19th century began the modern transformation of the shipyard, energy.

234
00:16:34,700 --> 00:16:39,820
As power is introduced, we see shipyards begin to take the physical form that we recognize as a

235
00:16:39,821 --> 00:16:45,340
shipyard today, space filled with shops for protecting the machinery that's used

236
00:16:45,341 --> 00:16:50,540
to produce the various parts of a vessel. First steam and then electricity

237
00:16:50,541 --> 00:16:55,960
powered the tools and mechanized jobs once done entirely by hand, such as the

238
00:16:55,961 --> 00:17:00,160
cutting of wood and timber for the vessels. All of the heavy wood that was

239
00:17:00,161 --> 00:17:04,480
shaped here at the mill was done with the aid of heavy machinery, most of which

240
00:17:04,481 --> 00:17:12,480
which was run from a belt and shaft system that came across the top of the mill building.

241
00:17:12,481 --> 00:17:15,960
This machine for shaping heavy timbers was known as the Daniels Planer.

242
00:17:15,961 --> 00:17:19,800
It had a circular cutting head that would go around at a great rate of speed and could

243
00:17:19,801 --> 00:17:23,300
take up off to two inches at a time.

244
00:17:23,301 --> 00:17:28,760
The chips flew with such force that they actually gouged out areas of the ceiling and walls

245
00:17:28,761 --> 00:17:31,420
of the mill all around them.

246
00:17:31,421 --> 00:17:35,820
But all in all, it was a fairly effective tool for cutting down very, very thick and

247
00:17:35,821 --> 00:17:37,740
heavy pieces of timber.

248
00:17:37,741 --> 00:17:43,580
The shipyard's ability to use power tools to shape and trim the lumber allowed for greater

249
00:17:43,581 --> 00:17:46,160
efficiency in shipbuilding.

250
00:17:46,161 --> 00:17:49,660
Other trades began to appear within the yard.

251
00:17:49,661 --> 00:17:54,320
Workers once hired on a per-ship basis found it more economical to set up shop right on

252
00:17:54,321 --> 00:17:56,480
the premises.

253
00:17:56,481 --> 00:18:01,120
Permanent design buildings such as mold lofts became part of the shipyard complex.

254
00:18:01,760 --> 00:18:06,300
There were literally dozens of different trades that went into the making of a wooden ship.

255
00:18:06,700 --> 00:18:12,420
And in a 19th century shipyard like this, it all began here in the mold loft where the design started to take place.

256
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:18,300
The designers would come up with a half model of the vessel, take its measurements,

257
00:18:18,301 --> 00:18:21,280
and blow them up full size here on the mold loft floor.

258
00:18:21,281 --> 00:18:28,200
that was in order to create patterns that could then be used to cut out the right-shaped timbers to build the schooners.

259
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:34,180
Every single piece of wood that went into the frame of the schooner had to be sketched out first.

260
00:18:34,720 --> 00:18:39,280
That's why the mold loft became such an integral part of a shipyard like Percy and Small.

261
00:18:41,420 --> 00:18:47,960
Carpenters overseen by master builders then began the arduous task of assembling the timbers that eventually became the ship's hull.

262
00:18:47,961 --> 00:18:55,840
Within the shipyard, workers constructed the frames on a platform attached to the keel.

263
00:18:55,841 --> 00:19:01,240
When a frame piece was complete, oxen or horses pulled the frame down to the emerging hull,

264
00:19:01,241 --> 00:19:06,840
where it was raised into position by animal power, using block and tackle.

265
00:19:06,841 --> 00:19:11,820
Countless tradespeople in turn added their skills to the growing vessel.

266
00:19:11,821 --> 00:19:18,220
Everyone from plankers and caulkers to sailmakers were necessary in the 19th century shipyard.

267
00:19:18,221 --> 00:19:22,420
But the greatest change for shipyards and builders came with the crossover from wood

268
00:19:22,421 --> 00:19:26,440
to metal in the late 19th century.

269
00:19:26,441 --> 00:19:31,700
Hewing of timber and sewing of sails, jobs that had been performed for millennia, would

270
00:19:31,701 --> 00:19:38,100
all but disappear as the world's oceans began to be traversed by floating steel.

271
00:19:38,101 --> 00:19:46,100
Riveters, torchmen and ironworkers began to populate the yards, each with their own tools and crafts.

272
00:19:46,101 --> 00:19:54,100
When you're dealing with riveter construction and steel, obviously the weight or displacement of these ships is going up dramatically.

273
00:19:54,101 --> 00:20:00,100
One of the key things they need to invent in the yards were cranes, something to pick up the heavy weight.

274
00:20:00,101 --> 00:20:07,100
They start out with steam powered cranes that really are called portal cranes that go around the side of the dry dock

275
00:20:07,101 --> 00:20:10,280
and have large arms with the hook on the end

276
00:20:10,281 --> 00:20:11,560
that they can pick things up.

277
00:20:11,860 --> 00:20:15,600
And then they eventually shift it over from steam to electric.

278
00:20:16,860 --> 00:20:19,300
The powerful cranes came just in time

279
00:20:19,301 --> 00:20:21,480
to lift shipyards into a new century,

280
00:20:22,100 --> 00:20:24,660
a century that would see both the triumph

281
00:20:24,661 --> 00:20:26,060
of the massive steel vessels

282
00:20:26,061 --> 00:20:29,980
and the stunning destructive power unleashed by shipyards

283
00:20:29,981 --> 00:20:32,840
over the course of two world wars.

284
00:20:32,841 --> 00:20:41,720
modern shipyards owed their very existence to the evolution of building materials and craftsmanship

285
00:20:41,721 --> 00:20:50,120
by the beginning of the 20th century shipyards had undergone an epic change

286
00:20:50,121 --> 00:20:55,840
the wooden ships that had brought the world closer together were now sailing into history

287
00:20:55,841 --> 00:21:04,000
industrialization and technology were replacing wood with steel the age of the mammoth metal ship

288
00:21:04,001 --> 00:21:12,820
had begun the thing about steel shipyards is that they do not come from wooden shipyards

289
00:21:12,821 --> 00:21:21,360
the guys that have spent their lives building wooden sailing vessels are not going to wake up

290
00:21:21,361 --> 00:21:25,520
one morning and go to a different shipyard and be able to build steel vessels.

291
00:21:25,521 --> 00:21:35,040
The workforce for shipyards that build steel vessels comes from the foundries and the

292
00:21:35,041 --> 00:21:36,040
boilermakers.

293
00:21:36,041 --> 00:21:40,240
The new materials demanded precise work.

294
00:21:40,241 --> 00:21:46,960
Designers and naval architects began to be shipyard regulars as the old days of shaving

295
00:21:46,961 --> 00:21:52,800
wood with hand tools gave way to industrial presses that cut metal to exact specifications.

296
00:21:53,980 --> 00:21:58,980
In the United States, the technology began to change the yards, but it would take the

297
00:21:58,981 --> 00:22:02,580
First World War to unleash the nation's true shipbuilding potential.

298
00:22:03,360 --> 00:22:07,643
When World War I began in August 1914, the U.S.

299
00:22:07,644 --> 00:22:11,380
had for years been depending on the ships

300
00:22:11,381 --> 00:22:16,500
of European nations, primarily England, Great Britain, but also France and Germany.

301
00:22:16,960 --> 00:22:20,000
to carry American commerce to the world.

302
00:22:21,600 --> 00:22:24,880
As foreign governments pressed their ships into military service,

303
00:22:25,920 --> 00:22:28,340
America needed to build overseas transportation.

304
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:33,660
As a result, the U.S. underwent a shipyard building boom.

305
00:22:39,180 --> 00:22:44,260
The foremost government project for shipbuilding in World War I

306
00:22:44,261 --> 00:22:46,840
was the installation at Hog Island,

307
00:22:46,960 --> 00:22:51,120
which is a kind of mudflats in the Delaware River near Philadelphia.

308
00:22:52,280 --> 00:22:55,540
And the government moved in with contractors.

309
00:22:55,760 --> 00:22:58,420
They're taking essentially an undeveloped space.

310
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:05,840
You had to bring in all the machinery to move metal pieces to the shops to support, to fabricate.

311
00:23:06,460 --> 00:23:10,540
The Hog Island site cost over $65 million to build.

312
00:23:11,480 --> 00:23:16,720
For workers, the biggest job was stabilizing the barren, muddy land that sloped into the water.

313
00:23:16,960 --> 00:23:24,620
steam-powered pile drivers pounded telephone pole sized logs into the muck for support

314
00:23:24,621 --> 00:23:34,080
the defining feature of the mammoth construction site was the building of 50 shipways able to

315
00:23:34,081 --> 00:23:42,000
accommodate vessels nearly 400 feet in length and over 50 feet wide on either side of the shipways

316
00:23:42,001 --> 00:23:47,880
permanent cranes were positioned to move the massive bulkheads and plate steel into position

317
00:23:47,881 --> 00:23:55,020
at the height of production over 30,000 shipyard workers manned the tools at Hog Island

318
00:23:55,021 --> 00:24:03,900
but despite their best efforts the shipyard had almost no impact on the war the first ship built

319
00:24:03,901 --> 00:24:11,280
at the Hog Island facility didn't slide down the ways until August 5th 1918 building a ship is a

320
00:24:11,281 --> 00:24:17,269
a very complex process. A ship is the biggest movable thing that humans make.

321
00:24:17,270 --> 00:24:18,280
The logistics

322
00:24:20,200 --> 00:24:26,320
of that construction process are extremely important.

323
00:24:26,321 --> 00:24:32,320
The Yards designers had envisioned Hog Island as a fast-paced cargo ship assembly yard,

324
00:24:32,321 --> 00:24:39,320
where pre-built boilers, engines, propellers, and shaped steel arrived on train cars from

325
00:24:39,321 --> 00:24:43,000
manufacturers around the country.

326
00:24:43,001 --> 00:24:44,960
Unfortunately, the erratic supply stream

327
00:24:44,961 --> 00:24:47,320
and simultaneous site construction initially

328
00:24:47,321 --> 00:24:50,280
lowered the shipyard's efficiency.

329
00:24:50,281 --> 00:24:52,520
It took up to a year to complete a vessel.

330
00:24:56,520 --> 00:24:59,180
CARGO SHIPS, the ones that were built for the war,

331
00:24:59,181 --> 00:25:02,120
were perfectly useful in commercial trade.

332
00:25:02,121 --> 00:25:06,660
So here's this flood of ships now made available after 1920

333
00:25:06,661 --> 00:25:08,660
to the private operators,

334
00:25:09,660 --> 00:25:12,160
driving down the price of hulls,

335
00:25:12,161 --> 00:25:15,660
because there are so many of them competing for the small market.

336
00:25:15,661 --> 00:25:18,660
The shipyards can't produce new ships,

337
00:25:18,661 --> 00:25:21,160
and the industry was in depression

338
00:25:21,161 --> 00:25:24,660
before the depression began in 1929.

339
00:25:24,661 --> 00:25:26,660
It went from bad to worse.

340
00:25:26,661 --> 00:25:28,660
Throughout the Great Depression,

341
00:25:28,661 --> 00:25:32,660
American shipyards closed at an alarming rate,

342
00:25:32,661 --> 00:25:35,160
while the ships built during the First World War

343
00:25:35,161 --> 00:25:37,160
became more decrepit with each voyage.

344
00:25:38,320 --> 00:25:41,380
During the 1930s, President Roosevelt,

345
00:25:41,381 --> 00:25:44,540
frustrated by America's failing shipyard industry,

346
00:25:44,541 --> 00:25:46,840
created the U.S. Maritime Commission

347
00:25:46,841 --> 00:25:48,820
to modernize the U.S. merchant marine.

348
00:25:50,440 --> 00:25:53,580
The commission worked to design a standardized set of ships

349
00:25:53,581 --> 00:25:57,580
to carry U.S. goods and oversaw the funding for shipyards

350
00:25:57,581 --> 00:26:00,140
to mass produce the new vessels.

351
00:26:00,141 --> 00:26:02,300
You go to the Maritime Administration and say,

352
00:26:02,301 --> 00:26:04,140
I'd like to build a new ship.

353
00:26:04,141 --> 00:26:04,970
They say fine.

354
00:26:04,971 --> 00:26:09,120
We will find out what it would cost to build that ship at world rates.

355
00:26:09,121 --> 00:26:15,160
That is to say, at the lowest cost, adequate shipyard in the world.

356
00:26:15,760 --> 00:26:19,440
Then we'll find out how much it would cost to build it at a U.S. shipyard.

357
00:26:20,520 --> 00:26:21,720
We'll pay the difference.

358
00:26:22,760 --> 00:26:26,300
The subsidies program began to resuscitate the merchant shipyards.

359
00:26:28,180 --> 00:26:31,140
Meanwhile, Congress, fearing a second great conflict,

360
00:26:31,141 --> 00:26:34,020
recognized the need to build more warships,

361
00:26:34,560 --> 00:26:35,920
as well as repair facilities,

362
00:26:36,380 --> 00:26:38,460
to take care of the Navy's wide-ranging fleet.

363
00:26:39,180 --> 00:26:42,080
An explosion occurred really starting in 1938

364
00:26:42,081 --> 00:26:43,840
throughout the World War II period,

365
00:26:43,960 --> 00:26:47,540
but the period between 1942 and 1944,

366
00:26:47,840 --> 00:26:52,500
26 huge graving docks were put in service.

367
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:55,520
In the entire previous history of the United States,

368
00:26:55,521 --> 00:26:58,400
only 29 graving docks have been put in service.

369
00:26:58,401 --> 00:27:01,600
and here in a period of two years, 26 graving docks.

370
00:27:02,040 --> 00:27:04,400
The new facilities sprang up on both coasts,

371
00:27:05,100 --> 00:27:07,760
as well as Puerto Rico, Panama, and Hawaii.

372
00:27:09,260 --> 00:27:13,000
Engineers designed the new docks with the mammoth warships of the day in mind

373
00:27:13,001 --> 00:27:16,960
and built some to repair multiple vessels simultaneously.

374
00:27:17,960 --> 00:27:21,620
These were 1,092-foot-long graving docks.

375
00:27:21,700 --> 00:27:22,820
They weren't 300 feet.

376
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:25,400
These were for the big ships, for the carriers.

377
00:27:25,401 --> 00:27:31,080
These straw docks were way over 100 feet wide, about 150 feet wide and over 40 feet deep.

378
00:27:31,580 --> 00:27:32,660
And they need a lot of them.

379
00:27:33,820 --> 00:27:41,080
New underwater concrete technology allowed workers to cut construction time on these new docks from years to months.

380
00:27:41,940 --> 00:27:43,820
The method is called the Tremie method.

381
00:27:44,440 --> 00:27:46,980
And basically it's a way of pouring concrete underwater.

382
00:27:47,620 --> 00:27:53,840
Before he had to actually put cofferdams up and pump all the water out and make it dry before he could lay the concrete.

383
00:27:53,841 --> 00:27:58,460
But this was a way they could actually dredge out the area for the graving dock,

384
00:27:58,980 --> 00:28:05,960
place the forms down underwater, then actually through pipes lay concrete underwater.

385
00:28:07,100 --> 00:28:12,453
Two of the Navy's graving docks were put to the ultimate test during the event that thrust the U.S.

386
00:28:12,454 --> 00:28:13,154
into war,

387
00:28:13,500 --> 00:28:15,760
the Japanese attack at Pearl Harbor.

388
00:28:20,140 --> 00:28:23,440
One of the errors that the Japanese made on December 7th,

389
00:28:23,441 --> 00:28:27,520
they didn't totally destroy the shipyard, specifically the dry docks.

390
00:28:27,521 --> 00:28:30,000
They did bomb the ships that were in the dry docks.

391
00:28:30,640 --> 00:28:33,520
Pennsylvania, the Downs, the Cassin were in dry dock one.

392
00:28:33,700 --> 00:28:37,060
They were all terribly damaged, but they didn't destroy the dry dock.

393
00:28:38,280 --> 00:28:42,940
Within weeks after the attack, shipyard workers were able to put numerous ships,

394
00:28:43,460 --> 00:28:46,880
including three heavily damaged battleships, back into service.

395
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:52,460
Two additional battleships, too badly damaged to repair, were stripped clean,

396
00:28:52,461 --> 00:28:57,640
and their equipment and weapons were installed in new hulls at the San Diego Naval Yard.

397
00:28:58,320 --> 00:29:04,540
There was 7,000 dry dockings during World War II utilizing all these dry docks.

398
00:29:04,660 --> 00:29:09,440
So you could see the importance of dry docks in the war effort really, I think,

399
00:29:09,500 --> 00:29:11,240
turned the tide during the Pacific War.

400
00:29:11,241 --> 00:29:14,320
It was one of the key factors that made these ships available out there

401
00:29:14,321 --> 00:29:16,540
was having these repair assets available.

402
00:29:16,541 --> 00:29:24,900
Some of the Navy's wartime repairs were performed in 142 newly built floating dry docks.

403
00:29:26,040 --> 00:29:29,880
Some were individual modules that could be towed almost anywhere.

404
00:29:31,720 --> 00:29:37,800
Part repair facility and part submarine, once fitted together, yard workers could open valves

405
00:29:37,801 --> 00:29:39,760
which flooded and submerged the structure.

406
00:29:40,240 --> 00:29:45,320
When the vessel was in place, water was pumped out of the dock, causing it to rise,

407
00:29:45,321 --> 00:29:47,760
leaving the ship high and dry.

408
00:29:49,280 --> 00:29:51,940
During the Second World War, repair became very important,

409
00:29:52,120 --> 00:29:54,280
especially for the Navy, for two reasons.

410
00:29:54,360 --> 00:29:56,060
One was to repair battle damage,

411
00:29:56,160 --> 00:29:59,720
but the other thing was the technology was changing so fast,

412
00:30:00,120 --> 00:30:03,200
for example, with radar and the electronic systems,

413
00:30:03,780 --> 00:30:06,640
that there was a tremendous amount of work

414
00:30:06,641 --> 00:30:08,820
that had to be done to keep the ships up to date

415
00:30:08,821 --> 00:30:11,800
so that the ships were technologically superior to our enemies.

416
00:30:11,801 --> 00:30:19,640
But while some shipyards labored to keep the fleet afloat, others were welding and lifting their way into the record books.

417
00:30:22,100 --> 00:30:29,980
The United States was in the middle of a shipbuilding boom, and the shipyards were being called on to back the nation's war at sea.

418
00:30:33,900 --> 00:30:39,260
The carnage and destruction of World War II brought a golden age to American shipyards.

419
00:30:41,800 --> 00:30:48,300
Over half a million workers, many of whom were women, produced over 5,000 vessels for the war effort.

420
00:30:50,080 --> 00:30:52,000
Military ships were a clear necessity.

421
00:30:53,040 --> 00:31:01,000
At peak production, one shipyard, the Bath Iron Works in Maine, was able to launch a new destroyer every 17 days.

422
00:31:01,420 --> 00:31:08,780
But the true pinnacle of wartime shipbuilding was reached with the over 440-foot-long cargo carriers known as Liberty Ships.

423
00:31:09,640 --> 00:31:14,540
In World War II, when we were building the Liberty ships, and every ship was the same,

424
00:31:15,640 --> 00:31:19,560
as they built more and more ships, the production workers got better and better

425
00:31:19,561 --> 00:31:22,800
because they found little ways that they could build the ship faster.

426
00:31:23,260 --> 00:31:27,940
By 1943, many yards were producing a Liberty ship every month.

427
00:31:28,760 --> 00:31:33,360
One ship, the Robert E. Perry, was constructed in a record four and a half days.

428
00:31:34,480 --> 00:31:36,860
Upon hearing of the speed at which they were produced,

429
00:31:36,861 --> 00:31:40,180
Hitler reportedly stated, we have lost the war.

430
00:31:41,940 --> 00:31:44,360
The shipyards themselves were little changed.

431
00:31:45,120 --> 00:31:47,920
But inside, a revolution had taken place.

432
00:31:48,600 --> 00:31:51,880
Shipyard workers relied on new modular construction techniques,

433
00:31:52,680 --> 00:31:56,200
taking prefabricated pieces of ships and welding them together,

434
00:31:56,740 --> 00:31:59,900
as opposed to using the older, much slower riveting process.

435
00:32:01,660 --> 00:32:05,660
But part of the Liberty ship's success was due to the use of technology

436
00:32:05,661 --> 00:32:07,700
developed over half a century earlier.

437
00:32:08,740 --> 00:32:12,220
The technology utilized for the massive shipbuilding program

438
00:32:12,221 --> 00:32:17,720
in World War II was predominantly late 19th century propulsion technology.

439
00:32:18,260 --> 00:32:23,780
Also, the hull forms and the metal shaping were essentially late 19th century.

440
00:32:25,620 --> 00:32:29,300
Cutting-edge technology often requires a steep learning curve.

441
00:32:31,460 --> 00:32:35,380
Yard workers familiar with tried-and-true old ship construction methods

442
00:32:35,381 --> 00:32:39,820
were able to assemble the vessels with relative ease and help win the war.

443
00:32:41,380 --> 00:32:45,400
But as with the end of World War I, when hostilities ceased,

444
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:50,460
the heightened wartime production of cargo ships nearly devastated a portion of the industry.

445
00:32:51,740 --> 00:32:56,880
With this enormous surplus of cargo-carrying ships at the end of World War II,

446
00:32:57,560 --> 00:33:02,320
the shipyards, again, had practically no business building cargo ships.

447
00:33:02,321 --> 00:33:06,320
But they were busy with the Cold War building military ships, naval ships.

448
00:33:06,321 --> 00:33:09,320
And so the shipyards were very busy,

449
00:33:09,321 --> 00:33:17,320
and the United States remained fully armed in naval terms after World War II.

450
00:33:18,320 --> 00:33:25,320
After the war, existing shipyards were redesigned to build and maintain ever larger ships.

451
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:31,320
Engineers relied heavily on the modular shipbuilding techniques pioneered during the war.

452
00:33:31,321 --> 00:33:39,160
But now the individual pieces were building-sized structures, being hoisted and lowered by massive overhead cranes.

453
00:33:40,480 --> 00:33:43,500
But there was a downside to building on an epic scale.

454
00:33:44,300 --> 00:33:48,080
When a mistake was made, it was usually disastrous.

455
00:33:53,260 --> 00:33:59,720
The size and complexity of these new vessels caused shipbuilders to rethink one of the oldest shipyard traditions.

456
00:33:59,721 --> 00:34:05,720
Historically, shipyards built ships on waves.

457
00:34:05,721 --> 00:34:14,720
And the wave was basically inclined so that I could use the force of gravity.

458
00:34:14,721 --> 00:34:18,720
When I released the ship from its holding condition, it would slide into the water.

459
00:34:18,721 --> 00:34:21,720
Well, if you think about trying to build something that's level,

460
00:34:21,721 --> 00:34:24,720
it's very difficult to do it if it's already on an incline.

461
00:34:24,721 --> 00:34:25,421
incline.

462
00:34:25,422 --> 00:34:30,980
So if you build basically a land level facility where everything is referenced to a flat

463
00:34:30,981 --> 00:34:34,723
surface, it's much easier to assemble everything.

464
00:34:34,724 --> 00:34:38,160
So in the 60s and the 70s, the shipyards have

465
00:34:38,161 --> 00:34:42,820
transitioned from these launching ways where the ship is going to go down at an angle into the

466
00:34:42,821 --> 00:34:48,451
water to vessels that I'm going to build on a flat surface and launch it.

467
00:34:48,452 --> 00:34:49,840
In many shipyards,

468
00:34:49,841 --> 00:34:56,280
dry docks became the prime building areas but that wasn't always the case

469
00:34:56,520 --> 00:35:02,520
specialized rail and lift systems began to be incorporated in many yards

470
00:35:02,521 --> 00:35:07,920
like a locomotive switching yard ships were built and repaired on side tracks

471
00:35:07,921 --> 00:35:13,400
the ships were then towed by tractors onto a hydraulic central track and moved

472
00:35:13,401 --> 00:35:16,680
almost anywhere in the yard

473
00:35:16,681 --> 00:35:21,020
The technology allowed the shipyards to keep pace with the changing business.

474
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:26,200
In the 1960s, shipyards did something called jumbolizing.

475
00:35:26,840 --> 00:35:32,320
I had ships from the Second World War that there were still good life left in the machinery,

476
00:35:32,680 --> 00:35:34,500
the propelling equipment of the ship.

477
00:35:34,860 --> 00:35:38,640
However, the cargo carrying capacity wasn't quite large enough.

478
00:35:38,641 --> 00:35:45,160
So we'd bring the ship into a shipyard and we'd basically cut the machinery away from the cargo section,

479
00:35:45,161 --> 00:35:50,340
throw the cargo section away, and install a new cargo section that was larger,

480
00:35:50,620 --> 00:35:53,320
that we had already built and was waiting for the ship to arrive.

481
00:35:55,800 --> 00:35:59,780
But some of the biggest changes for shipbuilders took place inside the ships.

482
00:36:00,640 --> 00:36:05,520
Within a decade after the war, the U.S. Navy began to use the same atomic energy

483
00:36:05,521 --> 00:36:10,620
that had so chillingly and effectively ended the conflict to power its new breed of ships.

484
00:36:10,621 --> 00:36:17,620
The shipyards of the 1950s and 1960s took responsibility for the nuclear vessels.

485
00:36:17,621 --> 00:36:24,900
The nuclear yards obviously had things they had to worry about that the others may not

486
00:36:24,901 --> 00:36:29,740
have to worry about, but specific craftsmen required people that were familiar with this

487
00:36:29,741 --> 00:36:30,505
type of work.

488
00:36:30,506 --> 00:36:34,860
It took, of course, increased training, and it took some special equipment

489
00:36:34,861 --> 00:36:39,080
that was available out the yards and additional security and that type of thing.

490
00:36:39,081 --> 00:36:39,781
But I think

491
00:36:39,782 --> 00:36:46,560
you'll find that all the naval shipyards had a similar type of set of skills and similar shop

492
00:36:46,561 --> 00:36:48,281
names and similar tools.

493
00:36:48,282 --> 00:36:53,660
America was now building some of the best warships the world had ever seen,

494
00:36:54,220 --> 00:36:57,440
and a handful of shipyards were pushing technology to the limits.

495
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:02,684
But the Navy's triumphs belied a serious problem. U.S.

496
00:37:02,685 --> 00:37:05,140
shipbuilding was on a steep decline,

497
00:37:05,141 --> 00:37:11,860
and over the next few decades, conspiring forces would push American shipyards to near extinction.

498
00:37:13,840 --> 00:37:20,540
At the start of the 1980s, America's shipyards were entering a period of production not seen since the Second World War.

499
00:37:22,000 --> 00:37:26,520
The Cold War had led to increases in both the U.S. and Soviet navies

500
00:37:26,521 --> 00:37:29,880
and America was about to raise the stakes yet again.

501
00:37:29,881 --> 00:37:34,880
In 1980, President Reagan came in and said,

502
00:37:34,881 --> 00:37:37,880
the Russian Navy is challenging our Navy.

503
00:37:37,881 --> 00:37:39,880
We're going to have a 600-ship Navy.

504
00:37:39,881 --> 00:37:42,880
This threw an enormous amount of construction

505
00:37:42,881 --> 00:37:45,880
to the shipyards that built for the Navy.

506
00:37:47,880 --> 00:37:52,880
The new ships were some of the most sophisticated ever built.

507
00:37:52,881 --> 00:37:56,880
Engineers began to rely on computers to both design

508
00:37:56,881 --> 00:37:58,880
and build the extremely complex vessels.

509
00:37:58,881 --> 00:38:05,880
Today, the computer's workhorse cousin, the robot, lives on shop floors at places like

510
00:38:05,881 --> 00:38:09,880
the Northrop Grumman Newport News Shipyard.

511
00:38:13,880 --> 00:38:18,880
This is the 6-axis ABB fully programmable robot.

512
00:38:18,881 --> 00:38:25,880
Its articulated arm moves similarly to a human's, but the welding torch at the tip does most of the work.

513
00:38:28,880 --> 00:38:32,020
Engineers download schematics directly into the machine,

514
00:38:32,340 --> 00:38:36,160
which then quickly welds the complex but often repetitive shapes.

515
00:38:40,500 --> 00:38:45,240
Machines like this have allowed shipyard builders to continue perfecting the efficient technique

516
00:38:45,241 --> 00:38:49,740
that originated during the First World War, modular construction.

517
00:38:51,400 --> 00:38:57,700
More and more, the interior portions of the ship, such as the engines, come in modules.

518
00:38:57,701 --> 00:38:59,860
The engine's already been tested at the factory.

519
00:39:00,080 --> 00:39:00,900
You already know it works.

520
00:39:01,440 --> 00:39:08,060
The radar system has been fully assembled and put together in New Jersey and tested.

521
00:39:08,460 --> 00:39:10,580
And then as a unit, you know it works.

522
00:39:10,680 --> 00:39:13,200
Then it's crated and shipped off to the shipyards.

523
00:39:15,640 --> 00:39:22,180
Today's shipyards are constantly fine-tuning their operations to maximize efficiency and maintain profits.

524
00:39:22,181 --> 00:39:28,180
The shipyard of the immediate future is going to look a lot like the shipyard of today.

525
00:39:28,181 --> 00:39:34,180
We're still going to build ships out of steel because steel is relatively efficient and it's relatively inexpensive.

526
00:39:34,181 --> 00:39:36,180
We're going to use welding technology.

527
00:39:36,181 --> 00:39:44,180
The real improvements in shipbuilding are going to come from the ability to plan and to integrate.

528
00:39:44,181 --> 00:39:47,240
that I will be able to take design information.

529
00:39:47,800 --> 00:39:49,760
That information will go to every vendor,

530
00:39:50,100 --> 00:39:54,540
and the vendors will know instantly everything that changes and how it affects them.

531
00:39:55,240 --> 00:39:59,960
But the shipyards of the future will depend on more than the savings brought on by proper planning.

532
00:40:01,060 --> 00:40:05,660
Throughout the 1980s, some shipyards, such as Newport News and the Bath Iron Works,

533
00:40:06,240 --> 00:40:09,320
thrived on substantial government orders for high-tech Navy ships.

534
00:40:10,660 --> 00:40:14,140
But Washington's actions nearly destroyed the rest of the industry.

535
00:40:14,180 --> 00:40:18,780
by cutting subsidies that had kept the merchant shipbuilders afloat since the Great Depression.

536
00:40:19,960 --> 00:40:24,440
The loss meant merchant shipowners now paid more for ships built in America,

537
00:40:24,980 --> 00:40:28,860
and predictably, they looked overseas for cheaper, more efficient yards.

538
00:40:30,280 --> 00:40:35,860
At the end of the Second World War, the Japanese and the Western Europeans

539
00:40:35,861 --> 00:40:38,440
had a great advantage over the United States

540
00:40:38,441 --> 00:40:42,580
because many of them were in the position that they could rebuild their shipyards.

541
00:40:42,581 --> 00:40:46,920
So basically they were starting with what you might think of as a clean sheet of paper,

542
00:40:46,921 --> 00:40:53,600
so that they could lay out everything in the most advantageous way to minimize the cost of production.

543
00:40:54,220 --> 00:40:59,860
For example, it takes about five times the number of movements in a U.S. shipyard,

544
00:41:00,240 --> 00:41:05,720
all of which survived the Second World War, as it does in a Southeast Asian shipyard.

545
00:41:06,660 --> 00:41:10,900
The one-two punch of lost subsidies and more efficient foreign yards

546
00:41:10,901 --> 00:41:13,520
have had a profound effect on American shipyards.

547
00:41:14,900 --> 00:41:18,340
When I graduated from the Naval Academy in 1980

548
00:41:18,341 --> 00:41:19,800
with a Naval Architecture degree,

549
00:41:20,180 --> 00:41:22,260
there are 22 major shipyards in this country

550
00:41:22,261 --> 00:41:23,540
involved in ship construction.

551
00:41:24,420 --> 00:41:26,360
Today, there are basically six.

552
00:41:26,361 --> 00:41:31,640
In 1982, we had about 112,000 people

553
00:41:31,641 --> 00:41:32,660
working in the industry.

554
00:41:33,400 --> 00:41:37,300
In 2002, production workers were about 45,000.

555
00:41:37,301 --> 00:41:43,660
As shipyards disappear from American shores, they have almost no chance of being rebuilt,

556
00:41:44,180 --> 00:41:48,500
since public facilities or housing tracts gobble up the valuable beachfront property.

557
00:41:49,720 --> 00:41:55,080
One of the largest shipyards in the United States, Hog Island, Pennsylvania, which was just south of Philadelphia,

558
00:41:55,420 --> 00:42:01,600
which was one of the most productive shipyards in the country in the 1920s, is now the Philadelphia International Airport.

559
00:42:01,601 --> 00:42:07,360
It would be very difficult to reclaim that back if you had to build a shipyard again.

560
00:42:07,361 --> 00:42:12,460
But the biggest loss will have little to do with machinery or dry docks.

561
00:42:12,461 --> 00:42:18,400
The biggest concern for those in the business today is what happens to the shipbuilders once

562
00:42:18,401 --> 00:42:19,552
the yards go away.

563
00:42:19,553 --> 00:42:24,160
There are some people that have been in those shipyards 20, 30, 40 years

564
00:42:24,161 --> 00:42:29,980
that are living encyclopedias on how you do things and how you do them right and they've

565
00:42:29,981 --> 00:42:32,740
You've seen what can go wrong.

566
00:42:32,741 --> 00:42:35,040
And when you lose that experience,

567
00:42:35,041 --> 00:42:37,320
you can't just pick it up off the street again.

568
00:42:37,321 --> 00:42:41,140
It takes 30, 40 years to build.

569
00:42:41,141 --> 00:42:43,880
Shipyards remain the places where builders use

570
00:42:43,881 --> 00:42:46,720
the world's largest tools to fashion

571
00:42:46,721 --> 00:42:49,440
the world's largest machines.

572
00:42:49,441 --> 00:42:51,840
But the loss of a shipyard is the loss

573
00:42:51,841 --> 00:42:53,820
of the age-old transfer of knowledge

574
00:42:53,821 --> 00:42:56,440
from master to apprentice.

575
00:42:56,441 --> 00:42:59,120
And that knowledge, once departed,

576
00:42:59,121 --> 00:43:01,120
may never find its way back to shore.

577
00:43:10,880 --> 00:43:12,400
There are always going to be surprises.

578
00:43:14,240 --> 00:43:17,040
You don't have to speak the language to be able to interact with people.

579
00:43:18,600 --> 00:43:21,920
Knowing that can make you look at history in a new and exciting way.

